steps.body:
Two patterns cover most dictionary work. To **count**, read the current count, default it to `0` with `??`, and add one:

```javascript
const counts = {};
for (const word of words) {
  counts[word] = (counts[word] ?? 0) + 1;
}
```

To **group**, give each key an array the first time you see it, then push into that array. `??=` assigns only when the current value is `null` or `undefined`, as it is for a missing key, and the expression gives back the array either way:

```javascript
const groups = {};
for (const word of words) {
  (groups[word.length] ??= []).push(word);
}
```

Newer JavaScript has `Object.groupBy`, but it does not exist in Node 20, so group by hand like this.

**The gotcha:** a plain object already inherits names such as `constructor` and `toString`. Counting the word `"constructor"` reads a built-in function instead of `undefined`, so `?? 0` never kicks in and the count turns into garbage text. The key `"__proto__"` breaks it too. That is fine for fixed lowercase data like the words here. For keys that come from input, use a `Map`, which the next lesson teaches.

example.code:
const fruits = ["apple", "pear", "apple", "fig", "pear", "apple"];
const counts = {};
for (const fruit of fruits) {
  counts[fruit] = (counts[fruit] ?? 0) + 1;
}
console.log(JSON.stringify(counts));

const words = ["bat", "apple", "bee", "cat", "ant", "cow"];
const byLetter = {};
for (const word of words) {
  (byLetter[word[0]] ??= []).push(word);
}
for (const [letter, group] of Object.entries(byLetter)) {
  console.log(`${letter}: ${group.join(", ")}`);
}

